Give the FrontierAccess global lock a lifetime that outlives teardown.

Any CORAL job that opened a frontier:// connection aborted with SIGABRT on
the way out, after its work was complete:

  terminate called after throwing an instance of 'boost::wrapexcept<boost::lock_error>'
    what():  boost: mutex lock failed in pthread_mutex_lock: Invalid argument

  coral::Context::~Context()                            <- from exit()
   coral::ConnectionService::ConnectionService::~ConnectionService()
    coral::ConnectionService::ConnectionPool::clearConnectionPool()
     coral::ConnectionHandle::~ConnectionHandle()
      coral::FrontierAccess::Connection::~Connection()
       coral::FrontierAccess::Connection::disconnect()  <- throws here

DomainProperties::s_lock was a namespace-scope static boost::mutex living in
liblcg_FrontierAccess.so; the Context that owns the connection pool is torn
down from exit() in liblcg_ConnectionService.so. Nothing orders the two, and
in practice the mutex was destroyed first - so pthread_mutex_lock on it
returned EINVAL, boost turned that into lock_error, and the exception escaped
~Connection(), which is std::terminate.

Making the mutex immortal removes the dependency on cross-library static
destruction order entirely. It leaks exactly one mutex, at process exit.

Reported here rather than upstream-only because CORAL is no longer maintained;
CMSSW carries the same code in cms-externals/coral.

--- a/FrontierAccess/src/DomainProperties.h
+++ b/FrontierAccess/src/DomainProperties.h
@@ -84,9 +84,6 @@
       /// The chunk size in bytes for a LOB retrieval
       int m_lobChunkSize;
 
-      /// The class global lock
-      static boost::mutex s_lock;
-
     };
 
   }
@@ -95,7 +92,20 @@
 
 inline boost::mutex& coral::FrontierAccess::DomainProperties::lock()
 {
-  return s_lock;
+  // Deliberately allocated and never deleted. This used to be a namespace-scope
+  // static boost::mutex in liblcg_FrontierAccess.so, while CoralKernel's
+  // Context is torn down from exit() over in liblcg_ConnectionService.so. By
+  // the time ConnectionPool::clearConnectionPool() got to
+  // FrontierAccess::Connection::disconnect(), which locks this, the mutex had
+  // already been destroyed: pthread_mutex_lock returned EINVAL, boost threw
+  // lock_error out of ~Connection(), and an exception leaving a destructor is
+  // std::terminate. Every job that opened a frontier:// connection aborted
+  // with 134 on the way out, after its work was already done.
+  //
+  // Leaking one mutex at process exit is the usual price for not depending on
+  // destruction order between shared libraries.
+  static boost::mutex* const s_lock = new boost::mutex();
+  return *s_lock;
 }
 
 
--- a/FrontierAccess/src/DomainProperties.cpp
+++ b/FrontierAccess/src/DomainProperties.cpp
@@ -8,8 +8,6 @@
 
 #include "CoralKernel/Context.h"
 
-boost::mutex coral::FrontierAccess::DomainProperties::s_lock{};
-
 coral::FrontierAccess::DomainProperties::DomainProperties( coral::FrontierAccess::Domain* service ) :
   m_service( service ),
   m_tableSpaceForTables(""),
